 |\nL200: 【91†Figure5b.sh】  | 【91†Figure5b.sh】  |    |\nL201: 【92†Figure6.sh】  | 【92†Figure6.sh】  |    |\nL202: 【93†Figure7.sh】  | 【93†Figure7.sh】  |    |\nL203: 【94†Figure7_genmatrix.sh】  | 【94†Figure7_genmatrix.sh】  |    |\nL204: 【95†LICENSE】  | 【95†LICENSE】  |    |\nL205: 【96†README.md】  | 【96†README.md】  |    |\nL206: 【97†gen_matrix.cpp】  | 【97†gen_matrix.cpp】  |    |\nL207: 【98†spmm-europar18-preprint.pdf】  | 【98†spmm-europar18-preprint.pdf】  |    |\nL208: [Button: View all files]\nL209: ## Repository files navigation\nL210: \nL211:   *   * 【99†README】\nL212:   * 【99†Apache-2.0 license】\nL213: \nL214: More items\nL215: \nL216: # Design Principles for Sparse Matrix Multiplication on the GPU: Sparse Matrix-Dense Matrix Multiplication\nL217: \nL218: by Carl Yang, Aydin Buluc, John D. Owens\nL219: \nL220: Accepted as Distinguished Paper at EuroPar 2018\nL221: ## Abstract\nL222: We implement two novel algorithms for sparse-matrix dense- matrix multiplication (SpMM) on the GPU. Our algorithms expect the sparse input in the popular compressed-sparse-row (CSR) format and thus do not require expensive format conversion. While previous SpMM work concentrates on thread-level parallelism, we additionally focus on latency hiding with instruction-level parallelism and load-balancing.\nL223: We show, both theoretically and experimentally, that the proposed SpMM is a better fit for the GPU than previous approaches. We identify a key memory access pattern that allows efficient access into both input and output matrices that is crucial to getting excellent performance on SpMM.\nL224: By combining these two ingredients—(i) merge-based load-balancing and (ii) row-major coalesced memory access—we demonstrate a 3.6× peak speedup and a 23.5% geomean speedup over state-of-the-art SpMM implementations on real-world datasets.\nL225: 【100†spmm-europar18-preprint.pdf】\nL226: ## Getting Started\nL227: \nL228: These instructions will get you a copy of the project up and running on your local machine for development and testing purposes.\nL229: \nL230: ### Prerequisites\nL231: \nL232: This software has been tested on the following dependencies:\nL233: \nL234:   * CUDA 8.0\nL235:   * Boost 1.58\nL236:   * CMake 3.11.1\nL237:   * g++ 4.9.3\nL238:   * ModernGPU 1.1\nL239: \nL240: * * *\nL241: ### CUDA 8.0\nL242: \nL243: If CUDA 8.0 is not already installed on your system, you will need to download CUDA 8.0 【101†here†developer.nvidia.com】. Follow the onscreen instructions and select the operating system and vendor that suits your needs. Download the 1.4GB file. You do not need to download the optional Patch 2.\nL244: After generating a download link, the commands I typed were the following:\nL245: \nL246:     `wget https://developer.nvidia.com/compute/cuda/8.0/Prod2/local_installers/cuda_8.0.61_375.26_linux-run\nL247:     chmod +x cuda_8.0.61_375.26_linux-run\nL248:     sudo ./cuda_8.0.61_375.26_linux-run\nL249:     `\nL250: \nL251: You will need to select:\nL252: \nL253:     `graphics driver: yes\nL254:     OpenGL: yes\nL255:     nvidia-xconfig: no\nL256:     CUDA 8.0: yes\nL257:     symbolic link: yes\nL258:     CUDA samples: yes\nL259:     `\nL260: Once installation has finished, check that your installation has completed by typing:\nL261: \nL262:     `nvidia-smi\nL263:     `\nL264: \nL265: If installation was successful, you should be able to see information about your GPU printed onscreen. Check that the right information has been added to your system path by typing:\nL266: \nL267:     `vi ~/.bashrc\nL268:     `\nL269: \nL270: If not already present, you should append to the bottom:\nL271: \nL272:     `export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$LD_LIBRARY_PATH:/usr/local/cuda-8.0/lib64\nL273:     export CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda-8.0\nL274:     `\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of aReusable Sparse GKR Engine (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14849v2)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 508\nL89: Parallel output policy  | complete local batch, depth 24, batch 768, 192 workers  | 14.10–14.64$\\times$ (higher because proving is faster)  | 9.71–10.71$\\times$ (6 Oct record)  | Fig. 【53†5】\nL90: Compact proofs  | depths 24–32, nine profiles  | unchanged in release 1.2  | 118 layers; +2.93% membership bytes  | Fig. 【51†6】\nL91: Deterministic proof bytes  | release 1.2 vs release 1.1, native and default builds, every serving mode  | 13,824 hash comparisons equal; every accepted reply in 187 replay-audited processes equals one of 576 reference proofs  | reference  | §【22†5】, §【23†6】\nL92: Tail latency  | 1,000 offered requests/s, same 192-core VM and day, three 60-s runs  | p99 33.7–33.9 ms (continuous)  | p99 433.8–475.1 ms  | Tab. 【54†4】\nL93: One 192-core VM  | every timed request within 1 s; one connection: three 60-s runs; two connections: 15-s screens  | 3,300/s on one connection, also for 30 min (5.94 million); $\\geq$5,000/s on two connections (frontend-limited lower bound)  | 1,000/s (7 Oct record); 1,150/s same day (one 60-s run), highest rate measured  | Fig. 【55†11】\nL94: One 48-core VM  | same budget, one 15-s screen per rate, same day  | 2,000/s, bound by worker CPU  | 500/s; 1,000/s fails  | Tab. 【56†5】\nL95: Overload memory  | 5,100 offered requests/s (1.5$\\times$ the highest one-connection screen pass), 2,760-MiB limit  | worker RSS at most 548,064 KiB sampled (402,948 KiB by GNU time); return budget full, no server refusal  | no admission limit in the library  | §【29†6.6】\nL96: Reusable core  | serving crate built on the public prover API only  | provided  | n/a  | §【12†2.7】\nL97: ## 2 Engine and application boundaries\nL98: \nL99: 【57†Figure 1】 contrasts the actual layer-oracle representations; 【58†Figure 2】 then separates shared preparation from request-specific proof state and original-request acceptance.\nL100: \nL101: Figure 1: SS-GKR layer reduction: six dense input-pair tables versus two-phase sparse booking. The representation explains the memory contrast; differential controls preserve round polynomials and proof results. The structure is unchanged in release 1.2.\nL102: ### 2.1 Layer reduction and structured wiring\nL103: Linear-time and structured GKR techniques already establish the algorithmic foundation [【43†11】, 【44†14】]. The production oracle uses a Libra-style two-phase sparse booking construction. Weighted linear and cube terms depend on the first input block. Multiplication supplies a sparse list of weighted input pairs. Summing the second block first produces a booking table; binding the first block then produces the remaining second-block coefficients.\nL104: This implementation avoids materializing the full input-pair hypercube.\nL105: For a layer with $n_{i}$ input wires, $n_{o}$ output wires and $m$ multiplication gates, the sparse auxiliary ownership is $O(n_{o}+n_{i}+m)$. The comparison baseline is StateSync-GKR’s own DenseLayerOracle, a test-only reference retaining the initial dense design. It constructs six $n_{i}^{2}$ factor tables and constructor temporaries. The production SparseLayerOracle implements Libra-style sparse reduction. Both use StateSync-GKR’s prove_impl driver and transcript order.\nL106: Thus Dense and Sparse name two representations within this implementation.\nL107: StateSync-GKR implements the sumcheck and GKR assembly from their published constructions; it uses Plonky3 0.4.3 for field arithmetic, hash and challenger primitives. The Dense reference is neither a Plonky3 GKR implementation nor the independently probed Lambdaworks GKR engine. The measured ratios isolate an internal representation change, not an external product comparison.\nL108: Verification has a separate optimization. TableWiring already iterates a sparse gate list. DerivedRegularWiring evaluates regular progression families in closed form and retains an exact sparse residue. Derivation checks each member’s progression, kind and coefficient; unsupported gates stay on the sparse path. Audit tests re-expand the representation and compare weighted gate multisets. This reduces repeated wiring work without changing the proof object.\nL109: It is distinct from replacing the quadratic prover oracle.\nL110: Release 1.2 reorganizes the arithmetic of the production oracle inside the same two phases. Output-wire weights come from scaled equality tables built once per layer instead of a product chain per output wire. Each phase computes the node values of a round polynomial in one pass over its table, taking two nodes from the table halves and the others by difference additions.\nL111: Round polynomials are interpolated with a constant inverse-Vandermonde matrix built once per layer, which removes the per-round inversions and small allocations of Lagrange interpolation.\nL112: Each change computes the same field values by an algebraically equivalent route, and field arithmetic is exact, so round polynomials and proofs are byte-identical to release 1.1: the committed test vectors match, the Dense and Sparse oracles still agree, and all 6,912 reference proof hashes of the benchmark corpus match in both the native and the default build (【22†Section 5】). Additional requested allocation at width 4,096 stays at 579,584 bytes.\nL113: Verification, the wiring oracles and the Dense reference are unchanged in release 1.2.\nL114: ### 2.2 Cube gates and path constraints\nL115: \nL116: The circuit intermediate representation supports weighted linear, product and cube gates. A Pow3 gate is an algebraic operation, not a processor instruction. The builder combines cube/linear taps and constants into one round layer. The Poseidon2 cube and matrix step are thereby represented without separate layers for every arithmetic subexpression [【59†3】]. The shipped primitives use the pinned Plonky3 0.4.3 registry dependencies [【45†8】].\n-------------------------------------