y collaborators\nL160: \nL161: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL162: \nL163: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL164: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? 【48†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL165: \nL166: 【49†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【50†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL167: \nL168: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【51†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL169: 【52†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【53†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【54†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【55†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【56†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【57†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【58†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【59†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL170: \nL171: Major funding support from\nL172: \nL173: 【60†(opens in new tab)†www.simonsfoundation.org】 【61†(opens in new tab)†www.sfi.org.bm】 【62†(opens in new tab)†www.schmidtsciences.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - Oraclizer/statesync-gkr: Rust prover for sparse-Merkle state transitions, with GKR and sumcheck mechanized in Isabelle/HOL · GitHub (https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr)\nciteturn7view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"lineno\":240}); Total lines: 523\nL220: ## Repository files navigation\nL221: \nL222:   *   * 【110†README】\nL223:   * 【110†Code of conduct】\nL224:   * 【110†Contributing】\nL225:   * 【110†License】\nL226:   * 【110†Security】\nL227: \nL228: More items\nL229: \nL230: 【111†Image: StateSync-GKR: sparse GKR proof engine for state synchronization】\nL231: # StateSync-GKR\nL232: \nL233: A Rust GKR and sumcheck engine for sparse-Merkle state transitions, with its protocol and compiler models machine-checked in Isabelle/HOL.\nL234: \nL235: 【55†Website†oraclizer.io】 · 【112†Documentation†oraclizer.io】 · 【113†Quick start】 · 【114†API guide】 · 【115†Measured profiles】 · 【100†Formal verification】 · 【116†Refinement evidence】\nL236: Its SMT frontend proves membership, non-membership and single-leaf updates. Models of the SMT circuit compiler and of the GKR and sumcheck protocol, and their composition, are machine-checked in Isabelle/HOL, building on the Archive of Formal Proofs `Sumcheck_Protocol` entry and including the exact degree-four KoalaBear extension field that the implementation uses.\nL237: A conditional, mechanized forward acceptance theorem and recorded proofs for selected Rust contracts relate the model to the shipped verifier: the checks that turn a request into an acceptance verdict (public-input binding, sumcheck rounds, carry construction and final input evaluations) can be traced across model and code; see 【116†Refinement evidence and reuse】.\nL238: Prepare a circuit once, reuse its proving and verification material, and run independent CPU proving jobs in parallel. The protocol and sumcheck crates have no dependency on the SMT compiler, so other arithmetic-circuit frontends can build on the same engine and use its 【116†acceptance-check review map】 when reviewing an integration.\nL239: ## Implementation benefits\nL240: 【117†Image: StateSync-GKR v1.2.0 in four measured cells, each with its own baseline: 89-fold less peak memory than the engine's own dense baseline on a width-4,096 test circuit (89.46-fold on v1.1.0, 88.87-fold on v1.2.0); 6,470-fold faster proving than that baseline on the same circuit, against 2,507-fold with v1.1.0; 3,300 requests a second on one 192-core server with every request accepted within one second, against 1,150 for v1.1.0 measured the same day; and a 99th-percentile latency of 34 ms at 1,000 requests a second on the same server, against 440 ms for v1.1.0 the same day.】\nL241: 【118†Image: StateSync-GKR v1.2.0 serving results against v1.1.0 measured the same day on the same VM types. On one 192-core VM at 1,000 requests per second, p99 latency from scheduled arrival to verified acceptance fell from 440 ms to 34 ms, medians of three 60-second runs. The highest offered rate with every timed request accepted within one second rose from 500 to 2,000 requests per second on 48 cores and from 500 to 3,000 on 96 cores, in short screens with one run per rate.\nL242: On 192 cores it reached 3,300 requests per second on one connection in three 60-second runs and a 30-minute run, against 1,000 for v1.1.0 in the earlier campaign and 1,150, the highest rate tried for it, on the same day. With two connections, 15-second screens passed every rate up to 5,000 requests per second, where the verifying frontend reached its own limit, so 5,000 is a lower bound.】\nL243: Version 1.2.0 keeps every proof byte and changes how proofs are computed and served. On one 192-core VM, the reference proof server in continuous mode had a p99 latency of 34 ms at 1,000 requests/s, against 440 ms for v1.1.0 measured the same day (medians of three 60-second runs), and accepted every request within one second at 3,300 requests/s on one connection in three 60-second confirmation runs.\nL244: In 15-second screens on 48-core and 96-core VMs, the highest offered rate with every request accepted within one second rose from 500 to 2,000 and to 3,000 requests/s. 【119†Performance and operating profiles】 gives each value with its server mode, host, offered rate, run length and connection count.\nL245: 【120†Image: Nine benefits in three groups. Use resources well: sparse reduction, cube and affine fusion, parallel Merkle constraints. Repeat efficiently: derived wiring, prepared reuse, independent jobs. Keep the meaning: three-state lifecycle, strict codec and request binding, reusable sumcheck/GKR core, selected Rust contracts and a conditional forward acceptance theorem.】\nL246:   * Use resources well: sparse reduction, cube/affine fusion and parallel Merkle constraints.\nL247:   * Repeat efficiently: derived wiring, prepared reuse and independent proof jobs.\nL248:   * Keep the meaning: three-state lifecycle, strict codec/request binding and a reusable core with traceable acceptance evidence.\nL249: 【121†See the implementation】 · 【122†Read the evidence conditions】 · 【115†Inspect the measurements】 · 【119†See the v1.2.0 serving results】 · 【123†Browse the performance overview†oraclizer.io】 · 【124†Read the design rationale†research.oraclizer.io】\nL250: \nL251: Measurements, structure, controls and model results support different claims. The nine mechanisms are not nine independently isolated causal experiments.\nL252: \nL253: Measurement conditions and the full implementation map\nL254: The engine combines sparse proving, a compact circuit schedule and reusable preparation. In separate controlled comparisons against its own references, StateSync-GKR used about 89 times less peak process RAM and achieved up to 23.68 times faster prepared verification.\nL255: Mechanism  | Application benefit\nL256: --- | ---\nL257: Two-phase sparse proving  | More circuit or independent-job capacity within a RAM budget\nL258: Derived wiring  | Less repeated work in the prepared verifier\nL259: Cube gates and affine fusion  | A compact representation of Poseidon2 rounds\nL260: Parallel Merkle constraints  | Modest proof-size growth across measured tree depths\nL261: Circuit, commitment and wiring reuse  | Lower cost for repeated requests of the same kind and configuration\nL262: Independent proof jobs  | Parallel CPU work with one result per original request\nL263: Empty, occupied and tombstone leaves  | Distinct absence, insertion, update, deletion and restoration semantics\nL264: Strict encoding and request binding  | Rejection of malformed or mismatched proof material\nL265: Reusable core and refinement evidence  | Build another arithmetic frontend on the same engine and use the model and the acceptance-check review map\n"
